# {{agent_name}}

{{agent_description}}

Built on [Anthropic Managed Agents](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/managed-agents/overview) using [nuvel](https://github.com/Folken2/nuvel).

The agent loop runs on Anthropic's infrastructure. This project is a thin proxy: a YAML agent definition, a setup script that registers it, and a FastAPI server that creates one session per incoming request.

## Quick start

```bash
# 1. Install
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt

# 2. Configure
cp .env.example .env
# Add your ANTHROPIC_API_KEY

# 3. Apply the agent and environment definitions (one-time)
python setup.py
# Writes MANAGED_AGENT_ID and MANAGED_AGENT_ENV_ID to .env

# 4. Local dev (no server)
python run_dev.py "say hello"

# 5. Server mode
python server.py
# → http://localhost:8000
# → POST /run_sse/ with {"prompt": "..."}
# → GET /health
```

## Project layout

```
agent.yaml                # what the agent IS — model, system, tools, MCP servers, skills
environment.yaml          # what container the agent's tools run in
setup.py                  # applies both YAMLs; persists IDs to .env
{{agent_package}}/
├── orchestrator.py       # session lifecycle: stream-first, idle-break gate, custom-tool dispatch
└── tools/
    ├── __init__.py       # custom-tool registry (host-side handlers)
    └── example.py        # one starter handler — replace
server.py                 # FastAPI: /run_sse/, /health
run_dev.py                # local CLI for testing without server
```

## Adding a custom tool

Two-step:

1. **Declare the schema** in `agent.yaml` under `tools:`:
   ```yaml
   - type: custom
     name: my_tool
     description: What it does and when to use it.
     input_schema:
       type: object
       properties:
         arg1: {type: string}
       required: [arg1]
   ```

2. **Implement the handler** in `{{agent_package}}/tools/`. Add a Python function that takes a dict of args and returns a string. Register it in `{{agent_package}}/tools/__init__.py` by adding to `_HANDLERS`.

Then run `python setup.py` to apply the new schema (creates a new agent version).

**Custom tools are the right place for any non-MCP API call that needs a secret.** The container has no env vars and vaults are MCP-only, so anything else (REST APIs with API keys, internal libraries, DB connections) lives here on your server.

## Adding an MCP integration

1. **Declare the server** in `agent.yaml` under `mcp_servers:` (no auth — just `type: url`, `name`, `url`).
2. **Create a vault** with the OAuth credentials (see [Vaults docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/managed-agents/tools.md#vaults)).
3. **Attach the vault to sessions** by passing `vault_ids=["vlt_..."]` in `orchestrator.py` `sessions.create()` call.

## Adding a skill

Anthropic prebuilt skills (`xlsx`, `docx`, `pptx`, `pdf`) — add to `agent.yaml` under `skills:`:

```yaml
skills:
  - type: anthropic
    skill_id: xlsx
```

Custom skills — create via the Skills API, then reference by ID. See [Skills docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/skills).

## Deployment

The `Dockerfile` and `railway.json` work out of the box. Set `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `MANAGED_AGENT_ID`, `MANAGED_AGENT_ENV_ID` (and optionally `API_KEY` for bearer auth) in the deployment environment. `MANAGED_AGENT_ID` and `MANAGED_AGENT_ENV_ID` come from the `setup.py` run on your local machine — copy them from `.env`.

For team workflows, prefer the [`ant` CLI](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/sdks/cli.md) which handles `create` / `update --version N` for the YAML files in CI.
